{
    "background_story": "In the 12th century, Josue was a wandering goliard, a medieval poet-musician who traveled from town to town sharing his art. With his lute slung over his shoulder, he would regale audiences with tales of love, chivalry, and the human condition. Josue's songs were infused with wit and satire, often poking fun at the clergy and the excesses of the nobility. Despite the risks of criticizing the powerful, Josue's clever wordplay and charming performances earned him a loyal following among the common folk. His music became a beacon of hope and social commentary, inspiring others to speak truth to power. Josue's legacy as a goliard lived on, influencing generations of troubadours and poets to come.",
